(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-allergy-inflammation-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Allergy, Inflammation & Dry Eye Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is meant by a soft steroid, and which agents are given as examples?","choices":["One given at a lower concentration — dexamethasone and prednisolone","One given by intravitreal injection — triamcinolone","Fluorometholone, loteprednol and rimexolone","One that does not cause cataract — difluprednate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Three of the listed agents are marked out as soft steroids for exactly that reason, which matters most in someone with a family history of pressure rise. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What should a patient starting cyclosporine drops be warned about?","choices":["Permanent darkening of the iris","An increased risk of cataract","Ocular burning, which affects about one in six","Rebound redness if the drops are stopped suddenly"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Burning occurs in roughly seventeen per cent, along with foreign body sensation and blurred vision. Warning about it up front is what keeps a patient on a drug that takes time to work. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"After placing a drop, the patient is told to close the eyes and press a finger over the lacrimal duct. What does that achieve?","choices":["It prevents the drop being diluted by reflex tearing","It limits drainage and systemic absorption","It spreads the drug evenly across the cornea","It reduces the stinging on instillation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Occluding the punctum stops the drop draining down the nasolacrimal duct, so more stays on the eye and less reaches the systemic circulation — the same principle as silicone plugs, done by hand for two to three minutes. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which cells are the principal targets in ocular hypersensitivity, and what happens when immunoglobulin E binds them?","choices":["Neutrophils and macrophages; binding triggers phagocytosis","T lymphocytes; binding triggers interleukin-2 release","Corneal epithelial cells; binding triggers prostaglandin release","Mast cells and basophils, within seconds"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Immunoglobulin E binds Fc receptors, tyrosine kinases activate within 5 to 15 seconds, and histamine, platelet-activating factor and leukotrienes are released — producing vasodilation, swelling, redness and itch. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"For which patients are ointments preferred over drops, and what must they be warned about?","choices":["Children and poor compliers; vision blurs for twenty minutes","Contact lens wearers, warned to keep lenses in to hold the ointment in place","Adults with glaucoma, warned that ointment raises pressure","Patients with dry eye, warned that ointment worsens tear film stability"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ointment works even if it only reaches the lashes, because it still makes contact with the eye — which is exactly why it suits a squirming child. The trade-off is blurring, so no driving until it clears. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which mediators are released in the ocular allergic response, and what do they produce?","choices":["Interleukin-2 and interferon gamma, producing scarring","Prostaglandins only, producing pain","Immunoglobulin E itself, producing direct vasodilation","Histamine, platelet-activating factor and leukotrienes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Those four signs are what every drug in this section is aimed at, and knowing which mediator each class addresses is what sorts antihistamines from stabilisers from steroids. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which three agents are listed as mast cell stabilisers?","choices":["Cromolyn, lodoxamide and nedocromil","Olopatadine, epinastine and azelastine","Ketorolac, nepafenac and diclofenac","Naphazoline, tetrahydrozoline and pheniramine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A short list worth holding because everything else in the allergy section is an antihistamine, a vasoconstrictor or an anti-inflammatory. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A patient has used an over-the-counter redness-relieving eye drop daily for a month. On stopping it, the eye becomes redder than before. What has happened?","choices":["Rebound hyperaemia, from downregulation of alpha-1 receptors","A new allergic conjunctivitis unmasked by stopping the drop","Tachyphylaxis, meaning the drug no longer works while it is being taken","Preservative toxicity from long-term use"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. These are alpha-adrenergic agonists that vasoconstrict to reduce redness and conjunctival oedema. Constant activation downregulates the receptors, so when the drug stops there are fewer left for endogenous noradrenaline and adrenaline to act on, and the vessels dilate. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"How does cyclosporine improve chronic dry eye?","choices":["It thickens the tear film to prolong contact time","It inhibits interleukin-2 production","It blocks cyclooxygenase in the lacrimal gland","It occludes the lacrimal drainage system pharmacologically"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Less inflammation in the lacrimal gland means increased tear production, with improved vision and comfort. Its indication is chronic dry eye associated with inflammation — keratoconjunctivitis sicca. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"How do ocular mast cell stabilisers work?","choices":["They inactivate the H1 receptor as inverse agonists","They inhibit phospholipase A2","They inhibit mast cell degranulation","They block cyclooxygenase"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. They stop the mediators being released at all, and also dampen release from basophils, eosinophils and neutrophils. That is prevention rather than blockade — and it is why they cannot rescue an acute attack. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A patient is being taught to instil an eye drop. What is named as the single most important step?","choices":["Washing the hands thoroughly with soap and water","Tilting the head back to form a lower lid pocket","Holding the eyes closed for two to three minutes","Covering the lacrimal duct after instilling"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is marked as the most important step, and it comes before anything else. The dropper must then not touch anything, so it stays sterile. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which adverse effect of ocular antihistamines is worth mentioning to a patient who already has dry eyes?","choices":["Raised intraocular pressure","Increased ocular dryness","Permanent iris colour change","Corneal ulceration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Along with irritation and headache, antihistamines can worsen dryness — which matters when dry eye is the background problem, since the treatment can aggravate the complaint. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"How do ocular nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs reduce inflammation?","choices":["They inhibit phospholipase A2, preventing arachidonic acid formation","They stabilise mast cells against degranulation","They block cyclooxygenase","They inactivate the H1 receptor"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. They act one step below the steroids: the steroids stop arachidonic acid being liberated at all, while the nonsteroidals stop what is already liberated becoming prostaglandin. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which adverse effects limit ocular glucocorticoid use, and how long is a course generally kept to?","choices":["Only transient burning, so courses may run for months","Rebound hyperaemia on withdrawal, limited to under two weeks","Permanent iris colour change, limited to a single dose","Cataract, raised pressure, infection and delayed healing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The pressure rise is greater with a family history. Because steroids suppress local immune function they also enable infection, which is why topical steroid use appears on the fungal keratitis risk list. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A toddler swallows a bottle of an imidazoline eye drop. Why does an agent that vasoconstricts locally cause central nervous system depression when ingested?","choices":["It acts on alpha-1 receptors both locally and systemically","Locally it acts on alpha-1 receptors, but systemically it targets alpha-2","It is metabolised to an opioid-like compound in the gut","It blocks rather than stimulates adrenergic receptors when absorbed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. That split is the whole reason the question is asked. Alpha-1 activation in the conjunctival vessels gives the local vasoconstriction; absorbed systemically the drug acts at alpha-2 receptors, and central alpha-2 agonism produces sedation, bradycardia and depressed respiration. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What do tear substitutes contain, and what is the purpose of the thickeners in them?","choices":["Only sterile water, the thickeners preventing evaporation","Antibiotic and thickener, to prevent secondary infection","Cyclosporine in a thickened base, to reduce inflammation","Electrolytes, surfactants and thickeners"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Same principle as a gel or ointment — the longer the preparation stays in contact, the more benefit it gives. Balanced salt solution, carboxymethylcellulose and polyvinyl alcohol are the over-the-counter examples. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 57"}]};
